00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:55.850
Unit 1: the gate and its order.

46
00:02:55.850 --> 00:03:00.229
Three pieces of the design meet here:
the Wave 2 administrator policies,

47
00:03:00.229 --> 00:03:05.350
the activation gate, and a custom authentication
strength for privileged sign-ins.

48
00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:08.803
Microsoft Learn's page on PIM
role settings sets the order,

49
00:03:08.803 --> 00:03:10.313
and the chapter quotes it.

50
00:03:10.313 --> 00:03:13.841
First, create and enable the
Conditional Access policy

51
00:03:13.841 --> 00:03:17.063
for a context before you
select that context in PIM.

52
00:03:17.063 --> 00:03:22.044
Second, if no policy targets the
context, PIM falls back to M F A,

53
00:03:22.044 --> 00:03:27.125
but not when the policy is off,
report-only, or excludes the user.

54
00:03:27.125 --> 00:03:30.825
Rule C A oh four warns when
a context policy isn't On.

55
00:03:30.825 --> 00:03:35.606
Third, don't scope the policy to both
the context and a directory role, because

56
00:03:35.606 --> 00:03:40.538
at activation the user doesn't hold the
role yet, so the policy would never apply.

57
00:03:40.538 --> 00:03:42.762
Rule C A oh five fails that.

58
00:03:42.762 --> 00:03:46.275
So this module builds the gate
and leaves it unselected.

59
00:03:46.275 --> 00:03:51.950
Module 4 selects C one only after this
module's run shows the gate is On.

60
00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:59.338
Unit 2: the check,
report-only, and the plan.

61
00:03:59.338 --> 00:04:02.367
The pre-merge check runs
rules C A oh one to

62
00:04:02.367 --> 00:04:05.663
oh nine on every policy
file before it merges.

63
00:04:05.663 --> 00:04:09.878
Some come from Microsoft Learn:
a context policy that isn't On,

64
00:04:09.878 --> 00:04:14.936
or that also targets roles; M F A and
an authentication strength together,

65
00:04:14.936 --> 00:04:17.634
or administrator roles with plain M F A;

66
00:04:17.634 --> 00:04:22.862
a policy that leaves out any of the
fourteen roles Microsoft Learn's page on

67
00:04:22.862 --> 00:04:26.824
phishing-resistant M F A for
administrators recommends;

68
00:04:26.824 --> 00:04:30.703
a context outside C one to C
twenty-five; and a report-only

69
00:04:30.703 --> 00:04:35.424
compliant-device policy that doesn't
exclude Android, i O S and mac O S.

70
00:04:35.424 --> 00:04:40.370
Others are the program's design: a
report-only instrument set to enabled,

71
00:04:40.370 --> 00:04:44.225
and a placeholder or a user
target that isn't an object I D.

72
00:04:44.225 --> 00:04:45.388
Oh one is both.

73
00:04:45.388 --> 00:04:49.585
On the repository as merged, the
check passes with five warnings,

74
00:04:49.585 --> 00:04:52.810
and they're rollout
decisions, not mistakes.

75
00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:59.910
Module 2 left a question
open, and here's the answer.

76
00:04:59.910 --> 00:05:05.173
Microsoft Learn doesn't require the
emergency exclusion on a report-only policy.

77
00:05:05.173 --> 00:05:10.172
The program's own pre-merge rule is
stricter: every policy excludes the group.

78
00:05:10.172 --> 00:05:12.247
The code sides with the stricter rule.

79
00:05:12.247 --> 00:05:17.766
It keeps the exclusion on every file,
except a named report-only instrument,

80
00:05:17.766 --> 00:05:23.373
such as C A oh nine oh, the policy that
records emergency sign-ins, which is Info.

81
00:05:23.373 --> 00:05:25.860
And two more choices make the writes safe.

82
00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:28.635
Every new policy is created report-only.

83
00:05:28.635 --> 00:05:32.898
Enforcing it is a separate change,
that needs the Allow Enforce switch.

84
00:05:32.898 --> 00:05:34.660
And the code never deletes.

85
00:05:34.660 --> 00:05:39.150
Rollback is changing a file's state,
to disabled, and applying it.

86
00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:44.700
The plan has seven actions.

87
00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:47.663
Create means the policy
isn't in the tenant yet,

88
00:05:47.663 --> 00:05:49.925
and it's always created report-only.

89
00:05:49.925 --> 00:05:54.301
Update means it differs, and the patch
carries only the changed top-level

90
00:05:54.301 --> 00:05:58.750
properties, as Microsoft Learn's
reference for updating a policy advises.

91
00:05:58.750 --> 00:06:00.587
NoChange means it matches.

92
00:06:00.587 --> 00:06:04.921
Blocked means it needs an authentication
strength or a context the

93
00:06:04.921 --> 00:06:09.176
tenant doesn't have yet, and the next
run, once that exists, creates it.

94
00:06:09.176 --> 00:06:11.229
Held means the file says enabled,

95
00:06:11.229 --> 00:06:14.425
the tenant doesn't, and you
didn't pass Allow Enforce.

96
00:06:14.425 --> 00:06:19.075
Differs is a strength that differs,
because the code doesn't update strengths.

97
00:06:19.075 --> 00:06:23.663
And NotInRepository is a policy in
the tenant that isn't in the files.

98
00:06:23.663 --> 00:06:25.837
It's reported, and never deleted.

99
